WEBVTT

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:07.620
Right. Watch this cell. I'm going to lift the salt bridge out, and the current drops to zero. Straight away.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:19.370
But the electrons don't even go through the salt bridge. They go through the wire. So what was that little tube actually doing? That's today's question.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:32.920
Look at the zinc half-cell first. Zinc atoms keep turning into zinc two plus ions, so positive charge builds up in that solution.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:46.300
Now the copper side. Copper two plus ions are being removed, because they're turning into copper. The nitrate ions get left behind, so negative charge builds up there.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:54.010
That build-up of charge pushes back against the flow. Within seconds, the current stops.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:05.760
Your turn to think. The salt bridge is potassium nitrate. Which ions move out of the bridge into the zinc half-cell? Pause the lesson and write it down.

00:01:08.757 --> 00:01:15.960
The nitrate ions. They're negative, so they move into the zinc half-cell, where positive charge is building up.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:33.190
And at the same time, potassium ions move into the copper half-cell, where copper two plus ions are being removed. Ions from each half-cell move into the bridge too. So ions move both ways.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:47.380
Now, how do you get the marks? The memo wants two functions. One: it completes the circuit. Two: it maintains electrical neutrality in the half-cells.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:02:00.100
If you have to describe it, name the ions and name the half-cells. Just writing that ions flow through the salt bridge gets you nothing on its own.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:17.280
Here's the one I see in every test. Electrons do not go through the salt bridge. Ever. Electrons stay in the wire and the electrodes. In the solutions and in the bridge, it's ions that carry the charge.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:33.590
Last thing: which salt do we use? The ions in the bridge must not react with either half-cell. That's why potassium nitrate is the usual choice. Nitrates don't form precipitates.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:52.290
Potassium chloride won't work next to a silver half-cell. The chloride ions react with the silver ions to form a white precipitate of silver chloride, and that removes silver ions from the cathode half-cell.

00:02:53.450 --> 00:03:06.310
Next to a permanganate half-cell it fails for a different reason. Permanganate is a stronger oxidising agent than chlorine, so it oxidises the chloride ions.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:21.080
Over to you. Open the galvanic cell lab, change the salt bridge, and watch what each choice does to the voltage. Then do the check questions. See you there.
